---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { formatUSD } from '../components/CurrencyInput';
import {
  ADDITIONAL_MEDICARE_RATE,
  ADDITIONAL_MEDICARE_THRESHOLD,
  MEDICARE_RATE,
  SOCIAL_SECURITY_RATE,
  SOCIAL_SECURITY_WAGE_BASE_2026,
} from '../lib/fica';
import { STANDARD_DEDUCTION_2026, TAX_BRACKETS_2026 } from '../lib/marginal-rate';
import { PARAMS_BY_YEAR } from '../lib/obbba-params';
import { OVERTIME_MULTIPLIER } from '../lib/paycheck';
import { formatLongDate, SITE_TAX_YEAR } from '../lib/site';
import { GRADUATED_REFERENCE_WAGE, STATE_TAX_DATA_AS_OF, STATE_TAX_LAST_UPDATED, STATES } from '../lib/state-tax-data';

// Every figure on this page comes from the constants the calculators use.
const pct = (r: number) => `${+(r * 100).toFixed(2)}%`;
const rates = TAX_BRACKETS_2026.single.map((b) => b.rate);
const bracketRange = `${pct(Math.min(...rates))} to ${pct(Math.max(...rates))}`;
const obbba = PARAMS_BY_YEAR[SITE_TAX_YEAR];

const summary: { label: string; value: string; note?: string }[] = [
  { label: 'Tax year', value: String(SITE_TAX_YEAR) },
  { label: 'Rules last checked', value: formatLongDate(STATE_TAX_LAST_UPDATED) },
  {
    label: 'State data',
    value: STATE_TAX_DATA_AS_OF,
    note: 'Some bracket tables show the latest year the state has published.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How We Calculate"
  description="The sources, assumptions, and limits behind NetWageTax's paycheck, tips and overtime, and state income tax estimates, and how to report an error."
  ogType="article"
>
  <PageHeader
    title="How we calculate"
    lead="Where our numbers come from, what they assume, and why they can differ from your actual paycheck or tax bill."
  >
    <dl class="mt-8 border-t border-line text-[15px] sm:text-base">
      {
        summary.map(({ label, value, note }) => (
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-0.5 border-b border-line py-2.5 sm:flex-row sm:justify-between sm:gap-6">
            <dt class="shrink-0 text-ink-2">{label}</dt>
            <dd class="text-ink sm:text-right">
              <span class="font-semibold">{value}</span>
              {note && <span class="mt-0.5 block text-sm text-ink-2">{note}</span>}
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </PageHeader>

  <article class="prose-page mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Federal income tax.</strong> The {SITE_TAX_YEAR} brackets ({bracketRange}) and standard deduction
        ({formatUSD(STANDARD_DEDUCTION_2026.single)} single, {formatUSD(STANDARD_DEDUCTION_2026.mfj)} married filing
        jointly, {formatUSD(STANDARD_DEDUCTION_2026.hoh)} head of household) come from the IRS inflation adjustments
        in Revenue Procedure 2025-32.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Social Security and Medicare.</strong> Social Security is {pct(SOCIAL_SECURITY_RATE)} of wages up to
        the {SITE_TAX_YEAR} wage base of {formatUSD(SOCIAL_SECURITY_WAGE_BASE_2026)}, published by the Social
        Security Administration. Medicare is {pct(MEDICARE_RATE)} of all wages, plus an additional{' '}
        {pct(ADDITIONAL_MEDICARE_RATE)} on wages above {formatUSD(ADDITIONAL_MEDICARE_THRESHOLD.single)}{' '}
        ({formatUSD(ADDITIONAL_MEDICARE_THRESHOLD.mfj)} married filing jointly).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tips and overtime deduction.</strong> The One Big Beautiful Bill Act (Public Law 119-21), which added
        sections 224 and 225 to the Internal Revenue Code, and the IRS instructions for Schedule 1-A. The deduction is
        capped at {formatUSD(obbba.tips.capSingleOrHoh)} for qualified tips and {formatUSD(obbba.overtime.capSingleOrHoh)}{' '}
        for qualified overtime ({formatUSD(obbba.overtime.capMfj)} married filing jointly), and phases out above{' '}
        {formatUSD(obbba.tips.phaseoutThresholdSingleOrHoh)} of modified AGI ({formatUSD(obbba.tips.phaseoutThresholdMfj)}{' '}
        married filing jointly).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>State income tax.</strong> Each state's tax agency. Every <a href="/state-taxes/">state page</a> links
        to its official source, and bracket tables name the document they were copied from.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="exact-vs-estimated">What's calculated exactly and what's estimated</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Calculated from the published rules:</strong> federal income tax on your taxable income, Social Security,
      Medicare (including the additional Medicare tax), and the tips and overtime deduction with its caps and
      phase-out. For the inputs you enter, these follow the law step by step.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Estimated:</strong> state income tax. We use one simplified formula for all {STATES.length}{' '}
      jurisdictions instead of each state's full tax return:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>States with no wage tax are $0.</li>
      <li>
        Flat-rate states apply their rate to wages above an approximate single-filer deduction or exemption.
      </li>
      <li>
        States with progressive brackets use one average rate, calibrated for a single filer earning about{' '}
        {formatUSD(GRADUATED_REFERENCE_WAGE)}, instead of running every bracket. Results are close for typical
        salaries and less accurate for much higher or lower incomes.
      </li>
      <li>
        The state estimate uses single-filer figures whatever filing status you choose, and it taxes your full wages:
        it doesn't subtract the federal tips and overtime deduction, which many states don't follow.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Paychecks can also differ from our figures because employers withhold using IRS Publication 15-T and your
      Form W-4, while we estimate the tax you owe for the full year.
    </p>

    <h2 id="assumptions">What the estimates assume</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You take the standard deduction rather than itemizing.</li>
      <li>
        No pre-tax deductions: no 401(k) or 403(b) contributions, health insurance premiums, or HSA contributions.
      </li>
      <li>No Form W-4 adjustments for dependents, other income, or extra withholding.</li>
      <li>No federal or state tax credits.</li>
      <li>No local income taxes from cities, counties, or school districts.</li>
      <li>
        In the paycheck calculator: 52 weeks of pay a year, overtime paid at {OVERTIME_MULTIPLIER}× your hourly rate,
        and no tips and overtime deduction (use the <a href="/tools/obbba-tax-calculator/">tips and overtime
        calculator</a> for that).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="review">How often we review the data</h2>
    <p>
      We update federal and FICA figures when the IRS and the Social Security Administration publish the next year's
      numbers, usually in October and November. We review the state data every January and whenever a state changes
      its law during the year. The date in the strip at the top of every page shows the last review.
    </p>

    <h2 id="report-an-error">Report an error</h2>
    <p>
      If a number looks wrong, <a href="/contact/">contact us</a> with the page, the values you entered, and what you
      expected to see. We check every report against the official source and fix confirmed errors.
    </p>
    <p>
      NetWageTax gives estimates for educational purposes, not tax advice. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
